♻️ What is Garbage Collection in Java?
Java uses automatic garbage collection to manage memory.
It frees up memory by removing objects that are no longer reachable or used by the application.

✅ You don’t need to manually delete objects — Java does it for you!
but you can also do manually by         ************   System.gc() ya Runtime.getRuntime().gc()   ******************

🔧 How Garbage Collection Works
The JVM tracks objects in heap memory.

If no references point to an object, it becomes eligible for GC.

The Garbage Collector runs in the background and cleans up unused objects.

🧩 Why is Garbage Collection important?
Prevents memory leaks

Ensures better performance

Reduces risk of OutOfMemoryError

🚀 Types of Garbage Collectors in Java (JVM)
| Collector                                    | Description                                           | Best For                                        |
| -------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| **Serial GC**                                | Uses a **single thread** to collect garbage           | Small apps or single-core systems               |
| **Parallel GC** (aka Throughput Collector)   | Uses **multiple threads** for GC                      | Applications that need **high throughput**      |
| **CMS (Concurrent Mark Sweep)** ❌ Deprecated | Reduces pause time by doing GC work **concurrently**  | Apps needing **low-latency** (like web servers) |
| **G1 GC (Garbage First)**                    | Splits heap into **regions** and collects in parallel | Large heaps, balances throughput + pause time   |
| **ZGC (Z Garbage Collector)**                | Low-pause GC for **very large heaps** (multi-GB)      | Apps with strict latency needs                  |
| **Shenandoah**                               | Similar to ZGC, developed by Red Hat                  | Ultra-low pause for large heaps                 |


🧪 Quick Comparison
| GC Type    | Multithreaded | Pause Time | Heap Size Support     | Use Case             |
| ---------- | ------------- | ---------- | --------------------- | -------------------- |
| Serial     | ❌             | High       | Small                 | Small apps           |
| Parallel   | ✅             | Medium     | Medium to Large       | Background jobs      |
| G1         | ✅             | Low        | Large                 | General-purpose apps |
| ZGC        | ✅             | Very Low   | Very Large (multi-GB) | Real-time systems    |
| Shenandoah | ✅             | Very Low   | Large                 | Enterprise apps      |


🔁 GC Phases (High-Level)
Mark – Identify live (reachable) objects.

Sweep – Remove unreachable (dead) objects.

Compact – Rearrange remaining objects to reduce fragmentation (in some GCs).

📦 Example: Make Object Eligible for GC
java
Copy
Edit
public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = new String("Hello");
        s = null; // Now eligible for GC
    }
}
